canon_reply: **Because the Earth’s axis is tilted, the two hemispheres point in opposite directions relative to the Sun as the planet orbits.**\n\n| What happens | Northern Hemisphere | Southern Hemisphere |\n|--------------|---------------------|---------------------|\n| **Summer** (around June 21) | Tilted toward the Sun → more direct rays, longer days → warmer. | Tilted away from the Sun → less direct rays, shorter days → cooler. |\n| **Winter** (around December 21) | Tilted away from the Sun → less direct rays, shorter days → cooler. | Tilted toward the Sun → more direct rays, longer days → warmer. |\n| **Equinoxes** (≈ March 21 & September 21) | Tilted neither toward nor away; both hemispheres receive roughly equal sunlight. | Same as the North. |\n\n### Why the opposite pattern?\n\n1. **Fixed axial tilt**  \n   - Earth’s rotation axis is inclined about **23.5°** relative to the plane of its orbit (the ecliptic).  \n   - This tilt is fixed in space; it doesn’t change as Earth moves around the Sun.\n\n2. **Orbit changes the Sun’s direction**  \n   - As Earth travels along its orbit, the Sun’s apparent position in the sky shifts.  \n   - When the northern hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun, the southern hemisphere is tilted away, and vice‑versa.\n\n3. **Resulting sunlight distribution**  \n   - The hemisphere pointing toward the Sun receives sunlight at a steeper angle and for longer daylight hours → summer.  \n   - The opposite hemisphere receives sunlight at a shallower angle and for fewer hours → winter.\n\nSo the seasons are “opposite” simply because the two halves of the planet are on opposite sides of the tilt axis at any given time. The geometry of the tilt and the orbit makes one hemisphere warm while the other cools, and the pattern reverses when the planet completes half an orbit.
